# 6 Debugging History & Failure Modes — ✅ High

- **Stale async processing:** A later CSV can supersede an earlier read/PDF generation. The controller uses a monotonically increasing token and destroys/revokes stale URLs.
- **Object-URL leakage:** The current URL is revoked before replacement, on stale completion, on teardown, and before unload.
- **Review leakage:** Any notice blocks PDF generation; no partial or “best effort” packet is emitted.
- **Unsafe closed-face content:** The model/PDF boundary keeps room numbers inside folds and on the internal index only.
- **Index overflow context:** Continuation pages repeat the internal-only security banner, reprint warning, timestamp, column headings, and pagination; preserve that behavior.
- **False focus proof:** A focus screenshot is insufficient unless the intended control visibly carries the focus indicator.
- **False release completion:** Local screenshots/tests do not prove publication, a final SHA, production behavior, provider behavior, payments, or demand.

# 7 Established Processes & Effective Patterns — ✅ High

- Start with the smallest end-to-end user contract and enforce it in controller tests and Playwright.
- Keep parsing, modeling, and PDF construction pure where possible; inject browser/time/PDF dependencies into the controller.
- Use fixed synthetic fixtures and an injected timestamp for deterministic PDF proof.
- Pair automated assertions with direct inspection of every release screenshot and rendered PDF page.
- Treat each browser project as a required gate, not optional coverage.
- Preserve immutable evidence between final validation and commit; rerun affected validation whenever proof or source changes.
- Keep shared lane/registry mutation separate from app-local implementation work.

